cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this framework, benchmark, or results in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "PINN Mechanistic Interpretability: Sparse Autoencoders, Causal Interventions, and Closed-Loop Control for Physics-Informed Neural Networks"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Khan
    given-names: Shamique
repository-code: "https://github.com/shamiquekhan/PINN-Mechanistic-Interpretability"
url: "https://github.com/shamiquekhan/PINN-Mechanistic-Interpretability"
abstract: >
  A research framework for analyzing, monitoring, and intervening on
  optimization failure modes in physics-informed neural networks (PINNs)
  using sparse autoencoders (SAEs), causal counterfactual interventions,
  causal-abstraction interchange tests, early-warning monitoring, and
  closed-loop adaptive control. Includes an 8-family PDE suite, a TopK SAE
  module with mandatory PCA/random/probe baselines, a
  multiple-comparison-corrected causal battery, a planted-feature positive
  control, a dependency-free Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) positive control
  that measures the low-rank/superposition regime boundary, SOTA
  optimization baselines, and a fully preregistered 13-stage reproducible
  pipeline. Headline finding: SAE-derived features provide no causal
  information beyond matched controls in this benchmark — a result that is
  basis-independent (PCA components fail the identical battery) — explained
  by activation geometry (participation ratio ~1.3 of 64 dimensions; local
  tangent rank exactly equals input dimension), with the regime boundary
  measured on both sides via the FNO positive control.
keywords:
  - physics-informed neural networks
  - mechanistic interpretability
  - sparse autoencoders
  - superposition
  - causal abstraction
  - scientific machine learning
  - negative results
  - reproducibility
